Z||000000||stingant0||12-07-2001||12:39 AM||||What is wrong with the TDC/DOHC? Consumer Reports thinks there is. They also think that just because a car designed in America and built here is unreliable. I've seen a Berretta with 300,000 miles on it. It still run good for not being touched. Sure Jap cars are reliable but I think America has caught up. <P>Car magizines do this also. It's always a Lexus or BMW that wins, never a STS. Why because the magiznes are partial to Forgein cars.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jaei Sun||12-07-2001||12:48 AM||||What DOHC cam engines do they think are crappy??  Almost every Foreign car manufactuer uses OHC as the arch quite succussfully.<P>GM really hasn't had a lot of really reliable DOHC designs until a few years ago with the 3.5 L DOHC (Short*) in the Olds sedans and the N*.  Hence why most of their vehicles have their "tried-and-true" OHV.<P>Hell Chrysler was (and still is) on the brink of being broken up by the Germans if they can't turn the company around...  But they can produce some decent DOHC designs- <P>2.4 L DOHC 4 (~150 HP) in the PT Crusier (sure it's underpowered but it's pretty reliable)<BR>2.7 L DOHC 6 (~200 HP) in the Sebring/Stratus 4-door<BR>3.5 (?) DOHC 6 (~253 HP) in the 300m<P>Now if a company that is in bad shape can produce some nice DOHC engines, why the hell can't the WORLDS BIGGEST COMPANY make some good sh!t??<P>I don't know about lexus but the reason why BMW always beats the STS is that BMW is far superior hands down.  I believe the best production cars you can buy on this planet are currently BMWs.  Have you seen the new 7 series?? Has the industry's first 6-speed automatic transmission.<P>To the best of my memory serves me, the Automatic transmission was "invented" by buick/oldsmobile way back and now look at em... nothing ground breaking anymore... pity.<P>And as for Consumer Reports when they reported the Montero Roll-over thing, Car and Drivel had a long-term tested one with somthing like ~20k miles on it and they didn't have a single roll over.  I don't put alot of faith in what Consumer Reports says...<p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||12:53 AM||||I dunno why.  Basicly, I get peoples opinions that have had first hand experience with what it is I am buying. Granted consumer reports is sometimes a good baseline, its certainly not the be all, end all say in the matter.  For that matter, the manner in which they collected their data could have been tainted. Or perhaps its just that the motor was somewhat of an ill fit for the car they put it in, so people get the impression it sucks. A 170HP motor in a 3500 lbs car is going to be dog slow and gutless. Whereas a 170HP motor in a 2800 lbs car, does ok.  In the former case peoples impression of the motor will be a negative one. Whereas in the latter it would probably be positive, even though it may be the exact same engine.||||reg||1
